<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>1.5.6: Practice</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00139</md:content-id>
    <md:title>1.5.6: Practice</md:title>
    <md:uuid>072d325c-8d65-47b5-9e7d-5e0d0f2679ac</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><para>Complete the following questions to practice the skills you have learned in this lesson.</para>
<para>Use the graph for questions 1 - 2.</para>
<figure><media alt="graph of (0, 4) and (4, 0)."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/aacd061405bc69664246ed8cf981961e9c6f1bb0"/></media></figure><div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="fdcb7998-4d0a-4abc-bf34-5b91f782d491" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="2" data-schema-version="1.0">
  <!--Q1-->
  <exercise data-content-id="3421aa35-b50d-4c5c-a3b1-441817c24a26" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\((0, 4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((2, 2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-2, 6)\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((0, 4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((2, 2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-2, 6)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-1, 3)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-4, 0)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which of the following ordered pairs are on the line? Select <emphasis>three</emphasis> answers.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((0, 4)\), \((2, 2)\), \((-2, 6)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.5.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q2-->
  <exercise data-content-id="415975ee-822f-40eb-99ca-a1679c41d927" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\((0, 4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((2, 2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-2, 6)\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((0, 4)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((2, 2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-2, 6)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-1, 3)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-4, 0)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which of the following ordered pairs are solutions to the equation? Select <emphasis>three</emphasis> solutions.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((0, 4)\), \((2, 2)\), \((-2, 6)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.5.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q3-->
  <exercise data-content-id="979c0e0d-259c-42cb-a28b-039132e7ceab" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\((0, -1)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((1, 2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((2, 5)\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((0, -1)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((1, 2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((2, 5)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((1, 1)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0, 1)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>For questions 3 and 4, use the graph of \(y = 3x -1\) as shown.</para>
      <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/2552427885cd3a872ea3566334a685b3e8fd6171"/></media></figure><para>Which of the following ordered pairs are on the line? Select <emphasis>three</emphasis> answers</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((0, -1)\), \((1, 2)\), \((2, 5)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.5.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q4-->
  <exercise data-content-id="9be3c94b-b5fd-4db2-adf0-8aa79239490f" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\((0, -1)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((1, 2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((2, 5)\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((0, -1)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((1, 2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((2, 5)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((1, 1)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0, 1)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which of the following ordered pairs are solutions to the equation? Select <emphasis>three</emphasis> solutions.</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((0, -1)\), \((1, 2)\), \((2, 5)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.5.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--Q5-->
  <exercise data-content-id="b0789d3d-017e-4fdf-9a8c-8a3f136c5cab" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\((-4, -3)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-1, 1.5)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0, 3)\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((-4, -3)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-1, 1.5)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0, 3)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((0, -2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((6, 4)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Which of the following ordered pairs are solutions to the equation? Select <emphasis>three</emphasis> solutions.</para>
        <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/b9f93f21031d9a609a1590b12847a90d908098d2"/></media></figure></problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((-4, -3)\), \((-1, 1.5)\), \((0, 3)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.5.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q6-->
  <exercise data-content-id="d5a98fb8-d5a5-4373-9325-cf4e7dd62f4d" data-problem-type="multiselect" data-solution="[&quot;\\((0, 2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((3, 1)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((6, 0)\\)&quot;]" data-solution-options="[&quot;\\((0, 2)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((6, 0)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((2, 6)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((3, 1)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((8, -1)\\)&quot;, &quot;\\((-1, 2)\\)&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>Here is a graph of the equation \( x+3y=6 \).</para>
      <para><emphasis>Select three</emphasis> coordinate pairs that represent a solution to the equation.</para>
      <figure><media alt=""><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4a347bae7c724f857dcffd3793112090bf5ce185"/></media></figure></problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! \((0, 2)\), \((3, 1)\), \((6, 0)\)</para>
    </solution>
    <div class="os-raise-ib-pset-encourage-response">
      <para>Try again. Revisit activity 1.5.3.</para>
    </div>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
